From: openSUSE packaging
Subject: Add allow_binary_downloads, off by default
Patch-mainline: No, not yet sent
References: https://github.com/zed-industries/zed

Zed fetches and runs code it did not ship with as soon as a file is
opened: built-in language servers missing from $PATH, the default
Prettier, and updates to every installed extension at start-up. It also
auto-installs the html extension. A distribution package should not do
that without the user asking for it.

allow_binary_downloads is a user-level setting (a project's
.zed/settings.json cannot set it) that gates all three. Built-in language
servers downloaded in an earlier session are not used while it is off.
Language servers provided by installed extensions are not covered:
installing the extension is the consent. Enabling it takes effect on a
language server's next start, and for the default Prettier and
extension updates after a restart of Zed. Disabling it takes full
effect after a restart of Zed: a server downloaded in the running
session stays cached until then.
Language servers found on $PATH or configured through
lsp.<name>.binary.path, a project's own Prettier, and extensions the
user installs or updates from the Extensions page keep working. A skipped
download names the setting, in the language server status and the log.

Upstream the default would stay true; the downstream parts are the false
default and the empty auto_install_extensions.

